                <!-- =====================================================
                     20. REAL WORLD APPLICATIONS
                     ===================================================== -->
                <h3 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-5 mb-3">
                    Real-World Uses of Multithreading
                </h3>

                <p class="text-muted mb-3">
                    Multithreading can be useful in applications that need concurrent
                    execution of independent work.
                </p>

                <ul class="text-muted mb-4">
                    <li>Desktop applications</li>
                    <li>Background processing</li>
                    <li>Logging systems</li>
                    <li>File processing</li>
                    <li>Data processing workloads</li>
                    <li>Server-side applications</li>
                    <li>Applications that perform multiple independent operations</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="alert alert-success mb-4">
                    <strong>CIIT Practical Point:</strong>
                    In real .NET projects, developers should understand threads,
                    synchronization and shared-state problems because concurrent
                    code can introduce difficult bugs if shared resources are not
                    handled correctly.
                </div>


                <!-- =====================================================
                     21. COMMON MISTAKES
                     ===================================================== -->
                <h3 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-5 mb-3">
                    Common Multithreading Mistakes
                </h3>

                <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                        1. Ignoring Shared Data
                    </h4>

                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Multiple threads modifying the same variable can create
                        unpredictable results.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                        2. Incorrect Locking
                    </h4>

                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Poor synchronization can lead to deadlocks, unnecessary
                        blocking, or performance problems.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                        3. Creating Too Many Threads
                    </h4>

                    <p class="mb-0">
                        Creating large numbers of threads can consume resources and
                        reduce application performance.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">
                    <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                        4. Assuming Output Order
                    </h4>

                    <p class="mb-0">
                        When multiple threads run concurrently, their output order
                        should not normally be assumed to be fixed.
                    </p>
                </div>


